, letters, words or base pairs according to the application. The n-grams typically are collected from a text or speech corpus. When the items are words, n-grams may also be called shingles.[1]  An n-gram of size 1 is referred to as a \"unigram\"; size 2 is a \"bigram\" (or, less commonly, a \"digram\"); size 3 is a \"trigram\". Larger sizes are sometimes referred to by the value of n, e.g., \"four-gram\", \"five-gram\", and so on.","topics":[],"score":213304,"global_rank":260285,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":41998},{"name":"mehuljoshi8/bean","owner":"mehuljoshi8","html_url":"https://github.com/mehuljoshi8/bean","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":0,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"Basketball Enters the Application Network","topics":[],"score":213302,"global_rank":260298,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":41999},{"name":"adamowada/class-29-demo","owner":"adamowada","html_url":"https://github.com/adamowada/class-29-demo","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":0,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":null,"topics":[],"score":213302,"global_rank":260300,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":42000}]